@h1 Tables h1@

Wit builds a table without making you wrestle a grid of pipes. You give it the
data in whichever form you already have — a small block of comma-separated rows,
a schema plus rows, or a collection of records you defined or loaded — and it
draws the table, header and all. Tables centre on the page by default.

@h2 The quickest table: inline rows h2@

Give the table a list of rows. The first row is the header; the rest are the
body:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Client Hours, COUN603, COUN704, Total], [One to One, 62.75, 41.25, 104], [Couple, 0.75, 0, 0.75]]| |caption Practicum Hours|
pre@@@

That renders — live, right here — as:

@table |rows [[Client Hours, COUN603, COUN704, Total], [One to One, 62.75, 41.25, 104], [Couple, 0.75, 0, 0.75]]| |caption Practicum Hours|

Each inner list is a row; commas separate the cells; the caption parameter adds a
centred, muted caption above the table.

@h2 A table from records h2@

The form that sets Wit apart: build a table straight from a collection of
records — data you defined once, or loaded at build time — with the columns drawn
from a schema. Define the records, then point the table at them:

@@@pre
#sites: [{ name - Dunmore Head, status - operational }, { name - Slyne Head, status - automated }] !!

@table |schema [name, status]| |rows @sites|
pre@@@

The schema names which keys become columns, in order, and each record becomes a
row. When you pass a record collection with _no_ schema, the columns are derived
from the keys of the first record — handy for a table straight off loaded data.

@h2 Every input form h2@

@h3 Inline rows h3@

A list of rows, header first: @@@code |rows [[H1, H2], [a, b]]| code@@@. The
simplest form, good for a handful of literal values.

@h3 Positional schema h3@

@@@code |schema [name, status]| code@@@ names the columns by key, in order, and
the keys double as the header labels. Pair it with a record collection in
@code rows code@. The headers are the raw keys — lowercase, exactly as written.

@h3 Labelled schema h3@

When you want display headers different from the keys, give each key a label:

@@@pre
@table |schema { name - Site, status - Status }| |rows @sites|
pre@@@

Now the header reads "Site" and "Status" while the data still keys off
@code name code@ and @code status code@.

@h2 Header and caption controls h2@

By default row zero is the header. You can override that:

@ul
@li _Suppress the header_ with @@@code |header false| code@@@ — the table renders
with a body only, no header row. li@
@li _Choose which row is the header_ with @@@code |header 1| code@@@ — row one
becomes the header and the rows before it are dropped. li@
@li _Set an explicit header_ with @@@code |header [Name, Value]| code@@@ — this
header is used and _every_ data row is kept as a body row. li@
@li _Add a caption_ with @@@code |caption Practicum Hours| code@@@ — a centred,
muted caption above the table. li@
ul@

@h2 Commas and multi-line cells h2@

Because commas separate cells, a comma _inside_ a value would split it into two
cells. When a cell needs to hold a comma — or span several lines — wrap its value
in a pair of exclamation marks:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Term, Meaning], [Kāwanatanga, ! The practice of good governance, spanning many contexts. !]]|
pre@@@

The wrapped cell keeps its comma (and any line breaks) inside a single cell.
There is no separate comma escape for record values, so the wrapper is the tool
to reach for whenever a cell's text contains a comma.

@h2 An empty table h2@

A table node with no parameters is legal and renders an empty table — useful as a
placeholder you fill in later.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Freezing a data-backed table._ The frozen literal form of the table (the
tripled-fence version) does _not_ resolve a record collection — it renders empty.
Always use the plain table node for any table backed by @@@code @records code@@@. li@
@li _A stray comma splitting a cell._ A comma inside a value creates an extra
cell. Wrap the value in the exclamation-mark cell wrapper (above). li@
@li _Expecting positional-schema headers to be prettified._ They are the raw
keys, lowercase. Use the labelled-schema form when you want display headers. li@
@li _Hand-aligning numbers._ The table centres on the page and leaves column
alignment to CSS; do not pad cells with spaces to align them. li@
ul@

